Ours (PhoneCanDo), not from termux-packages. Makes job control work under
proot: a job stopped by Ctrl-Z (SIGTSTP), SIGSTOP, SIGTTIN or SIGTTOU
stays stopped until SIGCONT, as it does without proot.

Without it, proot attaches with PTRACE_TRACEME. Such a tracee reports a
group-stop (the job really stopping) exactly like a signal-delivery-stop
(a stopping signal arriving), so proot restarts it like any signal stop
and the kernel drops the signal: the shell is told the job stopped and
shows it Stopped, but the job runs on. A background job reading the
terminal spins, stopped by SIGTTIN and restarted again and again. Upstream
knows it since 2014 (proot-me/proot#60, still open).

proot now attaches with PTRACE_SEIZE (Linux 3.4): the first tracee stops
itself before execve(2) as before, proot seizes it and wakes it with
SIGCONT; its children are seized too. A seized tracee reports a
group-stop as a PTRACE_EVENT_STOP carrying the stop signal, and proot
parks it with PTRACE_LISTEN: it stays stopped until SIGCONT (or SIGKILL)
brings another PTRACE_EVENT_STOP, a SIGTRAP one, after which it goes on
the way it went before. A parked job shows as "t (tracing stop)" in
/proc, not "T (stopped)"; its parent sees it stopped and continued as
without proot.

The rest of proot keeps its old view: the first stop of every tracee, now
a PTRACE_EVENT_STOP, is handed on as the SIGSTOP it used to be (the
start-up handshake, new children whose parent is not known yet); under
proot's ptrace emulation (strace, gdb in the guest) a group-stop reaches
the emulated ptracer as the bare stop signal and goes on when that
ptracer restarts it, and the stop a SIGCONT brings is never shown to it;
the POKEDATA workaround skips that stop too. PTRACE_LISTEN is never kept
as the way a tracee goes on (last_restart_how).

Applies on top of 0002-status-fd.patch (launch_process()). Worth offering
to termux/proot; drop it once termux/proot attaches with PTRACE_SEIZE.

